Publishing silently discards block editor segment values on culture varying document types#23553

## Description

cornehoskam

opened on Aug 4, 2026

Issue body actions

### Which Umbraco version are you using?

17.5.3

### Bug summary

Publishing a document silently discards the segment variant values of any Block List, Block Grid, Single Block or Rich Text property, when the document type varies by culture and the property itself does not vary by culture.

The values are written to the draft correctly and are visible in preview. They are simply never copied into the published version, so the front end renders the default segment instead. No error, no warning, and the backoffice reports no pending changes.

A non-block property with identical variance settings on the same document publishes its segment values correctly in the same publish action. That contrast is what makes the fault easy to isolate.

### Specifics

The fault is in the routing between two publish paths in `ContentRepositoryExtensions.PublishPropertyValues`:

    private static void PublishPropertyValues(IPublishableContentBase content, IProperty property, string? culture, PropertyEditorCollection propertyEditorCollection)
    {
        // if the content varies by culture, let data editor opt-in to perform partial property publishing (per culture)
        if (content.ContentType.VariesByCulture()
            && propertyEditorCollection.TryGet(property.PropertyType.PropertyEditorAlias, out IDataEditor? dataEditor)
            && dataEditor.CanMergePartialPropertyValues(property.PropertyType))
        {
            // perform partial publishing for the current culture
            property.PublishPartialValues(dataEditor, culture);
            return;
        }

        // for the specified culture (null or all or specific)
        property.PublishValues(culture);
    }

`PublishValues` has a `segment` parameter that defaults to `"*"`, and it iterates the varying values, so every segment gets published:

    public void PublishValues(string? culture = "*", string? segment = "*")
    {
        ...
        IEnumerable<IPropertyValue> pvalues = _vvalues.Where(x =>
                PropertyType.SupportsVariation(x.Value.Culture, x.Value.Segment, true) &&
                    (culture == "*" || x.Value.Culture.InvariantEquals(culture)) &&
                    (segment == "*" || x.Value.Segment.InvariantEquals(segment)))
            .Select(x => x.Value);

        foreach (IPropertyValue pvalue in pvalues)
        {
            PublishValue(pvalue);
        }
    }

`PublishPartialValues` has no segment parameter and never touches `_vvalues`. It publishes `_pvalue` only, which is the neutral/default segment:

    public void PublishPartialValues(IDataEditor dataEditor, string? culture)
    {
        if (PropertyType.VariesByCulture())
        {
            throw new NotSupportedException("Cannot publish merged culture values for culture variant properties");
        }

        culture = culture?.NullOrWhiteSpaceAsNull();

        var value = dataEditor.MergePartialPropertyValueForCulture(_pvalue?.EditedValue, _pvalue?.PublishedValue, culture);
        PublishValue(_pvalue, value);
    }

So every segment variant value on that property is dropped at publish time.

The opt-in condition is what pulls block properties into the broken path, and it is satisfied by exactly the configuration described here:

    // BlockListPropertyEditor, BlockGridPropertyEditor, SingleBlockPropertyEditor, RichTextPropertyEditor
    public override bool CanMergePartialPropertyValues(IPropertyType propertyType) => propertyType.VariesByCulture() is false;

Combined, the fault triggers when all of the following hold:

  1. The document type varies by culture.
  2. The property is a Block List, Block Grid, Single Block or Rich Text property.
  3. The property varies by segment but not by culture.

Files referenced, from the current 18 source tree, where the same logic is present:

  * `src/Umbraco.Core/Models/ContentRepositoryExtensions.cs` — `PublishPropertyValues`

* * *

### Steps to reproduce

On a clean Umbraco 17 install:

  1. Add a second language under Settings → Languages, so the site has two (for example English (United States) as default, plus Danish).

  2. Create a document type Home, and under its settings tick both Allow varying by culture and Allow segmentation.

  3. Add two properties to it, and on both tick Vary by segments and leave Vary by culture unticked:

     * `title` — Textstring
     * `blocks` — Block List, with any element type allowed
  4. Create a Home page, fill in `title` and add a block to `blocks`, and publish all languages. This gives you the default segment values.

  5. Now write a segment variant for both properties and publish. Any route works; this is the smallest:

    var content = contentService.GetById(homeId)!;

    // "test" is an arbitrary segment name
    content.SetValue("title", "segmented title", null, "test");
    content.SetValue("blocks", defaultSegmentBlockJson, null, "test");   // reuse the JSON saved in step 4

    contentService.Save(content);
    contentService.Publish(content, ["*"]);

  6. Run the query above against the Home node, or inspect `umbracoPropertyData` for the published version.

### Note on the document type variance

Step 2 matters. If Allow varying by culture is left off on the document type, `content.ContentType.VariesByCulture()` is false, the partial publishing branch is never taken, and both properties publish correctly. The bug needs a culture varying document with a culture invariant, segment varying block property.

* * *

### Expected result / actual result

Expected

After step 5, the published version holds the segment `test` value for both `title` and `blocks`, and the front end serves the segmented block content when that segment is active.

Actual

The published version holds the segment `test` value for `title` only. The row for `blocks` at segment `test` exists but its value is `NULL`.

Property  | Editor  | Draft  | Published
--- | --- | --- | ---
`title`  | Textstring  | segment value present  | segment value present
